def P(num,h,fig,tier,grant,inv,title,teaches,figs,status="Expired"):
    return dict(num=num,hash=h,fig=fig,tier=tier,grant=grant,inv=inv,title=title,teaches=teaches,figs=figs,status=status)
INTRO = "<p>Radio was invented almost entirely in the patent record. These drawings take you from the crystal detector a person can make at a kitchen table, through the spark transmitter, to the vacuum-tube circuits that made long-range voice radio possible. Use them with the crystal-set, antenna and receiver chapters of this guide.</p>"
UNPATENTED = ["<b>Heinrich Hertz's spark-gap transmitter and loop receiver</b> (1886&ndash;88), which first proved radio waves exist, were published as physics experiments and never patented.",
 "The WWII <b>'foxhole radio'</b> &mdash; a razor blade and pencil-lead detector &mdash; was a soldiers' improvisation, never patented. It works on the same principle as the crystal detectors below."]
PATENTS = [
P("US836531","8c/fb/e2/ce7f89c64b11ad/US836531.pdf","7f/da/af/b77d0e86fd91cb/US836531-drawings-page-1.png","A","1906-11-20","Greenleaf Whittier Pickard","Means for receiving intelligence communicated by electric waves",
  "The silicon crystal detector: a fine metal point pressed lightly on a crystal lets current pass mostly one way, turning the radio signal into sound in headphones with no battery at all. The heart of the crystal set and the 'cat's whisker'.","Detector stand with crystal cup and adjustable contact point; receiving circuit with aerial, tuner, detector and phones"),
P("US837616","f2/0b/55/e303c5b7115941/US837616.pdf","5a/3a/c8/b096256e89eb23/US837616-drawings-page-1.png","A","1906-12-04","Henry H. C. Dunwoody","Wireless-telegraph system",
  "The carborundum (silicon carbide) detector: a rugged crystal held between two contacts that needs little adjustment &mdash; a second proven crystal for a detector if galena or silicon is not to hand.","Detector clamped between contacts; circuit diagram of aerial, detector and telephone receiver"),
P("US586193","14/0d/c2/db3cbf4c0b7953/US586193.pdf","6b/f5/56/20677b68e93642/US586193-drawings-page-1.png","B","1897-07-13","Guglielmo Marconi","Transmitting electrical signals",
  "Marconi's spark system: an induction coil and spark gap feeding an elevated aerial and earth to send Morse, and a coherer receiver that responds to the waves. The first practical wireless telegraph &mdash; spark is very wide-band, so use it only in true emergencies.","Sheet 1: transmitter (coil, key, spark gap) and receiver (coherer, relay); sheets 2&ndash;3 in the PDF show aerial arrangements"),
P("US1113149","d7/d5/1b/36b6820d461abe/US1113149.pdf","0d/06/78/8f7f0b298faa3f/US1113149-drawings-page-1.png","B","1914-10-06","Edwin H. Armstrong","Wireless receiving system",
  "The regenerative receiver: part of a tube's output is fed back to its input, multiplying sensitivity many times so one tube does the work of several. The simplest high-gain receiver once you have a single tube.","Sheet 1: tube with tickler feedback coil and tuned circuits (sheets 2&ndash;3 show variants)"),
P("US803684","23/09/50/6d4980acdfed12/US803684.pdf","ea/10/6e/8db0fd1c3d656a/US803684-drawings-page-1.png","C","1905-11-07","John Ambrose Fleming","Instrument for converting alternating electric currents into continuous currents",
  "The Fleming valve (vacuum diode): a heated filament and a cold plate in an evacuated bulb pass current only one way &mdash; the first vacuum tube, used as a detector and rectifier.","Bulb with filament and plate; detector circuit"),
P("US879532","43/b2/02/128e1b322d46ea/US879532.pdf","d4/b1/ee/51484fef82bc2c/US879532-drawings-page-1.png","C","1908-02-18","Lee De Forest","Space telegraphy",
  "The grid Audion (triode): a zig-zag grid between filament and plate lets a small signal control a large current &mdash; the first amplifying device, the basis of every tube transmitter and receiver.","Tube with filament, grid and plate; receiving circuit with batteries and telephone"),
P("US1342885","08/f6/84/0f0ac124c0e3d2/US1342885.pdf","c0/c4/e2/4ed39ff685fab3/US1342885-drawings-page-1.png","C","1920-06-08","Edwin H. Armstrong","Method of receiving high-frequency oscillations",
  "The superheterodyne: the incoming signal is mixed with a local oscillator to a fixed intermediate frequency, where it is amplified and filtered. Still the layout of almost every receiver built today.","Block arrangement of local oscillator, mixer/detector, intermediate-frequency amplifier and second detector"),
P("US1647","bb/4a/53/3d3b9d7e4c8619/US1647.pdf","pdf:1","A","1840-06-20","Samuel F. B. Morse","Improvement in the mode of communicating information by signals by the application of electro-magnetism",
  "Morse's original 'American Electro-Magnetic Telegraph'. A signal lever opens and closes the circuit, and at the far end an electromagnet works a register that marks the signs on paper. The signs are dots, lines and spaces, measured out to stand for numbers and letters. Sheet 1 is Morse's own chart of the code. Use the code chart in this guide for sending today; the 1840 version on this sheet is not the code used on the radio now.","Sheet 1: Morse's code chart of dots, lines and spaces"),
P("US1083085","66/33/f9/34a46ec39f98d8/US1083085.pdf","e9/2a/d1/1ee0cbbd332ae3/US1083085-drawings-page-1.png","A","1913-12-30","Albert C. Gowing &amp; Millard C. Spencer","Loose-coupled tuning-coil",
  "A tuning coil for a wireless receiver, wound on rings that turn on a common rod. The primary and the secondary each have two sections that can be swung relative to one another, so you can adjust each coil's inductance and how tightly the two are coupled. It can also be switched to work as a close-coupled coil.","Fig. 1 front view of the rings; Fig. 2 section; Fig. 3 the loose- and close-coupled circuits"),
P("US174465","10/ab/a3/b4f5c6e5826640/US174465.pdf","e7/19/c0/1e6f0e4459819e/US174465-drawings-page-2.png","B","1876-03-07","Alexander Graham Bell","Improvement in telegraphy",
  "Bell's telephone patent. Sound sent as electrical undulations: an electromagnet with a steel-spring armature, and in Fig. 7 an armature moved by the human voice through a stretched membrane, so speech changes the current and a matching instrument at the far end reproduces it.","Sheet 2: Fig. 6, sets of electromagnets on one line; Fig. 7, the voice cone, membrane and armature at each end"),
]
